1
00:00:03,962 --> 00:00:06,923
[The Clarks'
"Better Off Without You"]

2
00:00:06,965 --> 00:00:13,304
♪ ♪

3
00:00:13,388 --> 00:00:17,976
- ♪ Oh-oh, oh-oh-oh ♪

4
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:19,978
♪ You are sultry, dirty ♪

5
00:00:20,061 --> 00:00:23,440
♪ Soft, and hard ♪

6
00:00:23,481 --> 00:00:27,110
♪ You are close to me,
and you're so far ♪

7
00:00:27,193 --> 00:00:29,863
[siren wailing]

8
00:00:29,946 --> 00:00:34,284
♪ And I'm thinking of the time
we spent together ♪

9
00:00:34,325 --> 00:00:38,621
♪ Now I'll bury this
in my backyard ♪

10
00:00:38,663 --> 00:00:41,124
[engine revving]

11
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:46,296
♪ Sometimes I sit and wonder ♪

12
00:00:46,379 --> 00:00:50,800
♪ If I'll never dial
your number ♪

13
00:00:50,884 --> 00:00:56,264
♪ 'Cause I'm having fun
looking out for number one ♪

14
00:00:56,306 --> 00:01:02,062
♪ And I'm doing all the things
I like to do ♪

15
00:01:02,145 --> 00:01:07,275
♪ I'm having fun
'cause I knew it all along ♪

16
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:11,654
♪ I'd be better off
without you ♪

17
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:14,532
♪ ♪

18
00:01:14,616 --> 00:01:16,533
- Hey, I need my cast off.

19
00:01:16,618 --> 00:01:17,827
- As soon as you
park that outside.

20
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:19,537
Morning, Doc.
- Olsen.

21
00:01:19,621 --> 00:01:20,997
Happy Fourth.

22
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:22,664
- Excuse me, guys.

23
00:01:22,707 --> 00:01:25,668
- Sorry.
[person coughing]

24
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:29,380
[indistinct chatter]

25
00:01:41,559 --> 00:01:43,019
[device beeps, door clicks]

26
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:46,106
[indistinct chatter continues]

27
00:01:50,276 --> 00:01:51,652
Hey, how's it going, Dad?

28
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:53,488
- [sighs]
I'm so sleep-deprived.

29
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:55,532
My house is starting to feel
like a CIA black site.

30
00:01:55,573 --> 00:01:57,491
- Have you considered hiring
a night nurse?

31
00:01:57,533 --> 00:01:59,202
- You're looking at him.
- You got a ripe one coming in

32
00:01:59,285 --> 00:02:00,870
you're gonna have to deal with
before everybody out there

33
00:02:00,954 --> 00:02:02,330
starts puking.
- Lovely.

34
00:02:02,372 --> 00:02:03,873
Your replacement came in early.

35
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:05,708
She brought in a full
bagel spread.

36
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:07,502
I'm just saying.
- She's not my replacement.

37
00:02:07,544 --> 00:02:09,044
She's just covering as chief
while I go

38
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:10,380
on my little sabbatical.
- Three months?

39
00:02:10,463 --> 00:02:11,965
- I'll be back
before your daughter

40
00:02:12,048 --> 00:02:13,800
sleeps through the night.
- Hey, dude, that's cold.

41
00:02:13,883 --> 00:02:15,552
- Morning.
- Good morning.

42
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:18,263
[elevator dings]

43
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:31,734
- Good morning, John.
- [sighs]

44
00:02:31,776 --> 00:02:33,736
I could have sworn
it was still around 4:00 a.m..

45
00:02:33,778 --> 00:02:35,488
- Did you guys have
an easy night last night?

46
00:02:35,572 --> 00:02:37,198
The waiting room
actually looks manageable.

47
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:39,075
- I wouldn't call it easy,
but not too bad.

48
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,244
I figured we'd have hands
and burns down here a bunch,

49
00:02:41,327 --> 00:02:43,371
but so far nobody's
blown off any fingers.

50
00:02:43,413 --> 00:02:45,248
- Oh, you know
you just jinxed it.

51
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:46,583
- [chuckles]
You guys and your jinxes.

52
00:02:46,666 --> 00:02:49,293
- Please, Jesus, please.
- Hail Mary again.

53
00:02:49,377 --> 00:02:51,296
- In the sanctified flesh.
- Don't let me die alone.

54
00:02:51,379 --> 00:02:54,591
- Just try to relax, Margaret.
- I need a priest.

55
00:02:54,632 --> 00:02:57,260
- Hey, hey, Dr. Robby.
- What's the good word, Perlah?

56
00:02:57,302 --> 00:02:59,137
- All your results came
back normal, Mrs. Walker.

57
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:00,138
- I'm dying.

58
00:03:00,221 --> 00:03:01,598
- You're not dying,
Mrs. Walker, I promise.

59
00:03:01,639 --> 00:03:02,974
- Jesus is coming for me.

60
00:03:03,057 --> 00:03:05,268
- If He is, He hasn't checked
in at registration yet.

61
00:03:05,351 --> 00:03:06,603
- Please don't let me die
a sinner.

62
00:03:06,686 --> 00:03:08,228
- Don't worry, Margaret--
we're going to have

63
00:03:08,271 --> 00:03:10,190
the hospital chaplain come
and see you in a moment.

64
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:11,691
- I'm not going
to last that long.

65
00:03:11,774 --> 00:03:13,526
- Yes, you will, because we're
going to give you a shot

66
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:14,944
to keep you alive
until they get here.

67
00:03:15,028 --> 00:03:16,613
What are you thinking,
Dr. Robinavitch?

68
00:03:16,654 --> 00:03:18,448
500 mikes of cyanocobalamin?

69
00:03:18,531 --> 00:03:20,617
- In her condition,
I'd push a full 1,000.

70
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,661
- Whoo.
You heard the man, Perlah.

71
00:03:23,745 --> 00:03:25,954
- God bless you.
God bless all of you.

72
00:03:26,039 --> 00:03:27,582
- Amen to that.

73
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:29,584
- Hail Mary, full of grace,
the Lord is with thee...

74
00:03:29,626 --> 00:03:31,419
- Ooh, nothing like
the miracle healing powers

75
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:32,921
of a good old B12 shot.

76
00:03:32,962 --> 00:03:36,049
How does she hold down a job?
- She doesn't.

77
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:37,716
- She's wearing
a waitress uniform.

78
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,302
- Yeah, from a diner
that closed 20 years ago.

79
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:41,596
- [over PA] Tier One Trauma,
ETA six minutes.

80
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:43,430
- And so it begins.

81
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:45,892
- My husband is still waiting
to see a doctor.

82
00:03:45,975 --> 00:03:47,143
- I apologize for the wait,

83
00:03:47,227 --> 00:03:48,978
but here are two doctors
just for you.

84
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:51,147
Mr. Burgess
has been complaining

85
00:03:51,189 --> 00:03:52,649
of leg cramps for a day.

86
00:03:52,690 --> 00:03:56,194
Slightly tachy at 110,
BP 128 over 75.

87
00:03:56,277 --> 00:03:58,154
Afebrile, good sats.

88
00:03:58,238 --> 00:04:00,156
- I'm Dr. Shen,
but I'm going off shift.

89
00:04:00,198 --> 00:04:02,367
Dr. Robinavitch will be one of
the doctors looking after you.

90
00:04:02,450 --> 00:04:03,660
- What other medications

91
00:04:03,743 --> 00:04:05,662
are you currently taking,
Mr. Burgess?

92
00:04:05,703 --> 00:04:07,747
- I brought his meds.

93
00:04:07,830 --> 00:04:09,791
- Thank you.
[dog whines]

94
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:11,334
- These are his supplements,

95
00:04:11,376 --> 00:04:13,503
mostly all-natural and organic
where possible.

96
00:04:13,544 --> 00:04:16,255
And these are
his homeopathic prescriptions.

97
00:04:16,339 --> 00:04:20,343
I also have a list
of his refrigerated probiotics.

98
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:23,012
[indistinct chatter]

99
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:25,014
- Where the hell is everybody?

100
00:04:25,056 --> 00:04:27,225
- Our new attending
is torturing

101
00:04:27,308 --> 00:04:29,519
our intern and med students
in Trauma One.

102
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:31,813
She came in early
to meet the night shift

103
00:04:31,854 --> 00:04:33,356
and took things over.

104
00:04:33,439 --> 00:04:36,317
- Okay, that takes giant balls
of disrespect.

105
00:04:36,359 --> 00:04:37,694
- Giant Balls of Disrespect

106
00:04:37,735 --> 00:04:39,487
was the name
of my band in high school.

107
00:04:39,529 --> 00:04:41,698
- [sighs] And my nickname
for my ex-husband.

108
00:04:41,739 --> 00:04:43,866
Hey, uh,
is this your last shift?

109
00:04:43,908 --> 00:04:47,829
- For a while, yeah.
- Oh, I am so jealous.

110
00:04:47,870 --> 00:04:50,498
Hey, I'll pay you a grand
if you take my second husband.

111
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:51,707
- Tempting.

112
00:04:51,791 --> 00:04:53,710
So tell me about
this new attending.

113
00:04:53,751 --> 00:04:56,838
- Uh, she's pretty,
divorced, one kid.

114
00:04:56,879 --> 00:04:59,382
Can already tell
she is a strict rule follower.

115
00:04:59,424 --> 00:05:01,259
I'm probably going
to grow to hate her.

116
00:05:01,342 --> 00:05:05,054
- What's her name, again?
- Dr. Baran Al-Hashimi.

117
00:05:05,096 --> 00:05:07,724
- Al-Hashimi?
What is that?

118
00:05:07,765 --> 00:05:10,059
- Irish?
- [chuckles]

119
00:05:10,143 --> 00:05:13,062
- She's some sort
of clinical informatics expert.

120
00:05:13,104 --> 00:05:14,897
Once she started
talking about AI,

121
00:05:14,981 --> 00:05:18,318
I started thinking about robots
and kind of stopped listening.

122
00:05:18,401 --> 00:05:20,820
- Clear.
Off the chest, Ogilvie.

123
00:05:20,903 --> 00:05:22,405
Get ready to step up here, Joy.

124
00:05:22,447 --> 00:05:24,615
Ogilvie, off the chest!

125
00:05:24,699 --> 00:05:26,075
- Clear!
[alarm blaring]

126
00:05:26,159 --> 00:05:27,910
- Still V-tach,
but it looks weird.

127
00:05:27,994 --> 00:05:29,704
- Agreed.
- No pulse.

128
00:05:29,746 --> 00:05:31,789
- No effusion,
good lung sliding.

129
00:05:31,873 --> 00:05:32,874
- Start compressions, Joy.

130
00:05:32,915 --> 00:05:33,957
- We haven't considered
all the Ts.

131
00:05:34,042 --> 00:05:35,960
- I checked for tamponade
and tension pneumo.

132
00:05:36,044 --> 00:05:37,211
- What if it's thrombosis?

133
00:05:37,253 --> 00:05:39,422
If it's a pulmonary or cardiac
clot, TNK could save him.

134
00:05:39,464 --> 00:05:41,507
- We are sticking
with the algorithm for now.

135
00:05:41,591 --> 00:05:43,551
- Algorithm's not working.
- Faster and deeper, please.

136
00:05:43,593 --> 00:05:46,429
- Uh, you need a break, Joy?
- No, I need an attending.

137
00:05:46,471 --> 00:05:48,765
- I'm right here.

138
00:05:48,848 --> 00:05:50,725
Stop compressions.
We're calling it.

139
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:52,185
[flatline]

140
00:05:52,268 --> 00:05:54,062
Sorry, the patient's dead.

141
00:05:54,103 --> 00:05:56,814
[flatline continues]

142
00:05:56,898 --> 00:05:59,400
All right.
Who can tell me what went well?

143
00:05:59,442 --> 00:06:01,110
- We followed
the V-tach algorithm.

144
00:06:01,152 --> 00:06:02,820
We checked
for reversible causes.

145
00:06:02,904 --> 00:06:04,614
- We limited our rhythm
and pulse checks to 10 seconds.

146
00:06:04,655 --> 00:06:05,948
- Excellent.

147
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,283
- Should we have
given thrombolytics?

148
00:06:07,325 --> 00:06:08,618
- No, you should have
recognized

149
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:10,787
that the cardiac rhythm
on the monitor was polymorphic,

150
00:06:10,828 --> 00:06:12,622
not just V-tach.

151
00:06:12,663 --> 00:06:14,540
It was torsades de pointes.

152
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:16,793
That's French--
"twisting of the points."

153
00:06:16,834 --> 00:06:18,628
- From prolonged QT,
antiarrhythmics,

154
00:06:18,711 --> 00:06:19,962
electrolyte disorders.

155
00:06:20,046 --> 00:06:21,255
- Your patient would have lived

156
00:06:21,297 --> 00:06:22,965
if you had pushed
magnesium sulfate.

157
00:06:23,007 --> 00:06:24,801
- Good news is
he's an organ donor.

158
00:06:24,884 --> 00:06:26,594
Parts of him are going to get
used to make Frisbees

159
00:06:26,636 --> 00:06:28,805
and water bottles and end up
as microplastics

160
00:06:28,846 --> 00:06:30,473
in our brains
and reproductive organs.

161
00:06:30,556 --> 00:06:32,141
- Thank you for the words
of encouragement, boss.

162
00:06:32,225 --> 00:06:33,810
- Dr. Al-Hashimi,
Dr. Michael Robinavitch.

163
00:06:33,851 --> 00:06:35,770
Everybody calls me Robby.
- Baran, please.

164
00:06:35,812 --> 00:06:37,355
- Welcome to the Pitt.
- Yeah, about that--

165
00:06:37,438 --> 00:06:38,773
- Trauma's a minute out.

166
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:40,691
- Ooh, who wants to treat
a real patient?

167
00:06:40,775 --> 00:06:42,151
- Yes, please.

168
00:06:42,193 --> 00:06:44,070
This thing's going
to give me nightmares.

169
00:06:45,822 --> 00:06:47,782
- I hope you don't mind me
jumping right into things.

170
00:06:47,824 --> 00:06:49,492
I find that a mock code
is a great way

171
00:06:49,575 --> 00:06:51,284
to assess
the students' capabilities.

172
00:06:51,327 --> 00:06:53,830
- Well, it might have been nice
if we'd had a little time

173
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:55,623
to go over how we do things.

174
00:06:55,665 --> 00:06:58,626
- Did you not get a packet
from Dr. Underwood? Gloria?

175
00:06:58,668 --> 00:07:00,670
It outlined
the quality-improvement changes

176
00:07:00,753 --> 00:07:02,505
I instituted at the VA.

177
00:07:02,588 --> 00:07:03,840
We're adopting the program

178
00:07:03,881 --> 00:07:05,633
at all
of the university hospitals.

179
00:07:05,675 --> 00:07:08,010
You didn't read it?
- No, I did. I mean, I will.

180
00:07:08,052 --> 00:07:09,512
I'm going to.
I want to.

181
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:11,180
I thought I had
a little bit more time

182
00:07:11,264 --> 00:07:13,182
before you came in today.
- We've already distributed

183
00:07:13,224 --> 00:07:15,685
patient passports
to everyone at registration.

184
00:07:15,726 --> 00:07:18,187
- What is a patient passport?

185
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:20,773
- So glad you asked.

186
00:07:20,857 --> 00:07:22,650
Samira?
- Dr. Al.

187
00:07:22,692 --> 00:07:25,278
- We worked together
at the VA a few years ago.

188
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:27,196
And now you're an R4.
- Yes.

189
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:29,323
And I'm so excited
you're joining us.

190
00:07:29,365 --> 00:07:30,741
Dr. Mel King is here, too.

191
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:32,201
I think you worked
with her as well.

192
00:07:32,243 --> 00:07:33,870
- Incoming!
- I did indeed.

193
00:07:33,953 --> 00:07:35,872
- John Doe--dishwasher,
penetrating trauma

194
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:37,874
from a kitchen knife
to the right chest.

195
00:07:37,915 --> 00:07:39,332
- Assault or accident?
- Unclear.

196
00:07:39,375 --> 00:07:42,044
Initially had a pulse,
lost it two blocks away.

197
00:07:42,086 --> 00:07:44,380
[respirator hissing]

198
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:46,466
- What do we got, party people?

199
00:07:46,549 --> 00:07:49,677
- Dr. Yolanda Garcia,
trauma surgery fellow,

200
00:07:49,719 --> 00:07:52,555
meet Dr. Baran Al-Hashimi,
new E.D. attending.

201
00:07:52,597 --> 00:07:54,390
She's gonna be covering for me
while I'm gone.

202
00:07:54,474 --> 00:07:55,975
- So nice to meet you.

203
00:07:56,058 --> 00:07:57,477
- About time they replaced you,
Rabbit-bitch.

204
00:07:57,560 --> 00:07:59,228
- I'm gonna miss you,
too, Yolo.

205
00:07:59,312 --> 00:08:01,564
Dr. Garcia has a hard time
expressing her feelings.

206
00:08:01,647 --> 00:08:02,607
- Not all of them.

207
00:08:02,690 --> 00:08:06,110
- One, two, three.

208
00:08:06,194 --> 00:08:09,447
Prep for a left thoracotomy.
- I'm on it.

209
00:08:09,530 --> 00:08:11,699
- What's up, White Chocolate?

210
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:15,536
Did you know our farm boy here
is a vintage-funk freak?

211
00:08:15,578 --> 00:08:17,914
Unfortunately,
he also likes to use

212
00:08:17,955 --> 00:08:20,416
other people's toothbrushes.
- It was one time.

213
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,084
They were the same color.

214
00:08:22,126 --> 00:08:24,086
- What's your plan, Samira?
- Dr. Mohan, next steps?

215
00:08:24,128 --> 00:08:25,421
- Chest tube to the right.

216
00:08:25,463 --> 00:08:27,173
Stand by with massive
transfusion protocol.

217
00:08:27,256 --> 00:08:28,424
- I put two units
on the infuser.

218
00:08:28,508 --> 00:08:29,759
- Sterile gloves, 6 1/2.

219
00:08:29,842 --> 00:08:30,885
- Here, we let the fellows

220
00:08:30,927 --> 00:08:32,260
and the residents
kind of take charge.

221
00:08:32,345 --> 00:08:34,429
- I'll hold on to them
just in case.

222
00:08:34,514 --> 00:08:37,433
- Is the VA even
a trauma center?

223
00:08:37,517 --> 00:08:41,062
- Not for medic runs,
but they walk in--

224
00:08:41,102 --> 00:08:45,107
falls, major MVCs, GSWs.

225
00:08:45,149 --> 00:08:46,609
- Stab wound's on the right.

226
00:08:46,651 --> 00:08:48,152
Why are we opening
the left chest?

227
00:08:48,236 --> 00:08:50,112
- Better access
if it's tamponade,

228
00:08:50,154 --> 00:08:51,989
and you can cross-clamp
the aorta.

229
00:08:52,073 --> 00:08:53,616
- Javadi, Whitaker,
sterile gloves.

230
00:08:53,699 --> 00:08:54,742
- Can I glove up?

231
00:08:54,784 --> 00:08:57,286
- It's best if you
watch the first one.

232
00:08:57,370 --> 00:08:59,080
- This guy's dead anyway.

233
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,126
- 10 blade.
- 28 French on a long Kelly.

234
00:09:05,294 --> 00:09:06,796
- Javadi, you're on suction,

235
00:09:06,837 --> 00:09:09,715
Whitaker, get ready
for internal compressions.

236
00:09:09,799 --> 00:09:11,467
[cell phone chimes]

237
00:09:11,551 --> 00:09:13,469
- Sorry, that's my phone.

238
00:09:15,054 --> 00:09:16,556
- Finochietto retractor.

239
00:09:16,639 --> 00:09:17,890
Whitaker,
give me a little room.

240
00:09:17,974 --> 00:09:19,141
- I need to get to his heart.

241
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:20,768
- I'm in.
Hook up the Thora-Seal.

242
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:22,144
[cell phone chimes]

243
00:09:22,228 --> 00:09:23,479
- You want me
to get that for you?

244
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:25,648
- Not right now.

245
00:09:25,690 --> 00:09:29,193
[hose slurping]

246
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:36,075
- No tamponade,
pretty dry in here.

247
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:39,161
Heart's empty.

248
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:40,830
Somebody start cardiac massage,
I'll cross-clamp.

249
00:09:40,871 --> 00:09:41,998
- Yeah, I got it.

250
00:09:42,039 --> 00:09:43,374
You do these one-handed,

251
00:09:43,457 --> 00:09:45,459
pushing up against the
breastbone with four fingers.

252
00:09:45,501 --> 00:09:47,378
- Very good.
- Not much blood to suction.

253
00:09:47,461 --> 00:09:50,006
- Okay, team, I think we need--
- Samira, next steps?

254
00:09:50,047 --> 00:09:51,048
- Keep transfusing.

255
00:09:51,132 --> 00:09:52,550
Open the right chest.
- That is correct.

256
00:09:52,633 --> 00:09:54,343
Booties on, kids.
There will be blood.

257
00:09:54,427 --> 00:09:56,721
[indistinct chatter]

258
00:09:56,804 --> 00:09:58,681
- Frank?

259
00:10:00,182 --> 00:10:03,227
Langdon.

260
00:10:03,311 --> 00:10:05,438
Frank?

261
00:10:05,521 --> 00:10:08,024
- [exhales deeply]

262
00:10:08,065 --> 00:10:10,860
[indistinct chatter]

263
00:10:12,528 --> 00:10:14,614
- You should be good
to go now, hon.

264
00:10:14,697 --> 00:10:16,699
- Thanks, Lupe.
- Of course.

265
00:10:16,782 --> 00:10:18,659
Hey, go get 'em.

266
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:23,998
- Hey, Doc.

267
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:27,001
It's been a while.

268
00:10:27,043 --> 00:10:30,630
I got a-a bad toothache today.

269
00:10:30,713 --> 00:10:33,299
- We'll take care
of that, Louie.

270
00:10:33,382 --> 00:10:34,717
- All right.

271
00:10:39,472 --> 00:10:42,516
[device beeps, door clicks]

272
00:10:59,116 --> 00:11:01,077
- [clears throat]

273
00:11:01,118 --> 00:11:04,163
[chatter continues]

274
00:11:08,084 --> 00:11:10,252
- There's too much blood
to suction.

275
00:11:10,294 --> 00:11:12,254
Uh, retracting the lung.

276
00:11:12,296 --> 00:11:14,590
- Try to identify the source.
- Could be the right ventricle.

277
00:11:14,674 --> 00:11:16,592
- Or more likely, the hilum.

278
00:11:16,634 --> 00:11:18,344
- Fresh lap pads.

279
00:11:18,427 --> 00:11:19,595
[cell phone chimes]

280
00:11:19,679 --> 00:11:23,015
- Siri, turn on silent mode!

281
00:11:25,309 --> 00:11:29,522
[keypad beeping, locker buzzes]

282
00:11:46,330 --> 00:11:48,915
[keypad beeping]

283
00:11:51,752 --> 00:11:53,337
[locker buzzes]

284
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,270
- [spits]

285
00:12:13,566 --> 00:12:15,109
- Good morning.

286
00:12:15,151 --> 00:12:16,819
- If it was a good morning,
I'd still be in bed.

287
00:12:16,902 --> 00:12:18,487
- [chuckles]
So what are you doing here?

288
00:12:18,571 --> 00:12:20,489
- Benji's mom is visiting
for the holiday.

289
00:12:20,573 --> 00:12:22,491
- She's still alive?
- That woman's too mean to die.

290
00:12:22,575 --> 00:12:24,160
Plus, who else is going
to get this place

291
00:12:24,201 --> 00:12:25,953
through
the Fourth of July weekend?

292
00:12:25,995 --> 00:12:27,747
- Sure as hell
ain't going to be me.

293
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:30,082
- I rest my case.
What am I walking into?

294
00:12:30,166 --> 00:12:31,667
- Nothing too bad, yet.

295
00:12:31,709 --> 00:12:34,670
Uh, these are the ones
I'd keep an eye on.

296
00:12:34,712 --> 00:12:38,674
[gasps]
The prodigal son returns.

297
00:12:38,716 --> 00:12:39,842
Too soon?

298
00:12:39,884 --> 00:12:42,303
- No, I guess not.

299
00:12:42,344 --> 00:12:44,972
You're back.
- I took some time.

300
00:12:45,014 --> 00:12:48,184
I'm like an old trail horse--
I always find my way back home.

301
00:12:48,225 --> 00:12:49,894
You look good, kid.
How you feeling?

302
00:12:49,977 --> 00:12:51,854
- Uh, yeah, great.

303
00:12:51,937 --> 00:12:54,482
You know, it's been a...

304
00:12:54,523 --> 00:12:56,984
it's been a journey.
- I bet.

305
00:12:57,026 --> 00:12:58,652
- Thanks.
Robby around?

306
00:12:58,694 --> 00:13:00,529
- Uh, he's in with a trauma.

307
00:13:00,613 --> 00:13:02,990
But if you hurry back, you can
catch some of the pass-ons.

308
00:13:03,032 --> 00:13:05,284
They're in North.
- Uh, thanks.

309
00:13:07,036 --> 00:13:09,079
- I'll keep an eye on him.

310
00:13:11,207 --> 00:13:13,375
- I think we have
a lost little lamb.

311
00:13:13,459 --> 00:13:16,045
Uh, excuse me, hon.
Can we help you?

312
00:13:16,086 --> 00:13:18,005
- I'm looking
for the charge nurse.

313
00:13:18,047 --> 00:13:20,049
- Oh, well,
you found two of 'em.

314
00:13:20,132 --> 00:13:21,509
I'm Lena.
This is Dana.

315
00:13:21,550 --> 00:13:22,802
I'm night shift, going home,

316
00:13:22,885 --> 00:13:25,137
but Dana is queen
of the day shift.

317
00:13:25,221 --> 00:13:27,223
- Emma Nolan.
Nice to meet you both.

318
00:13:27,264 --> 00:13:29,683
Uh, I'm a new grad RN
here to shadow.

319
00:13:29,725 --> 00:13:31,227
- Welcome to the Pitt,
Emma Nolan.

320
00:13:31,268 --> 00:13:32,895
Here, let me put some
of your stuff behind the desk,

321
00:13:32,978 --> 00:13:35,731
and I'll give you a quick tour.
- Okay.

322
00:13:35,773 --> 00:13:38,234
- Where you from, sweetheart?
- Sault Ste. Marie.

323
00:13:38,275 --> 00:13:39,693
- Oh.

324
00:13:39,735 --> 00:13:42,238
- It's in Upper Michigan,
practically Canada.

325
00:13:42,279 --> 00:13:45,074
- My great-grandmother
was from up that way--

326
00:13:45,115 --> 00:13:48,244
nice area, a lot of bugs.

327
00:13:48,285 --> 00:13:50,079
How about a cup of coffee?

328
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:51,413
[hose slurping]

329
00:13:51,497 --> 00:13:53,040
- Can't locate the source.

330
00:13:53,082 --> 00:13:54,792
- Proximal hilum
is under the sternum.

331
00:13:54,875 --> 00:13:56,794
- Along with most
of the right ventricle.

332
00:13:56,877 --> 00:13:59,588
- You need better exposure.
- Convert to a clamshell.

333
00:13:59,672 --> 00:14:02,049
- Exactly.
- Trauma shears.

334
00:14:02,091 --> 00:14:04,635
- Would've been nice to let
them come up with that plan.

335
00:14:04,718 --> 00:14:06,762
- Heart's going to need double
compressions just like this.

336
00:14:06,804 --> 00:14:08,097
[cell phone chimes]

337
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:10,599
- Squeeze from the apex
to the aorta.

338
00:14:10,641 --> 00:14:12,101
[hissing]

339
00:14:12,184 --> 00:14:13,769
- Right ventricle looks good.

340
00:14:13,811 --> 00:14:15,187
- Massive hemorrhage
from the hilum.

341
00:14:15,271 --> 00:14:18,732
- Second MTP pack is going,
additional four units.

342
00:14:18,774 --> 00:14:21,110
- Hilar injury
looks very proximal--

343
00:14:21,193 --> 00:14:23,362
may be pulmonary artery
and vein.

344
00:14:23,445 --> 00:14:26,073
- Clamp's on.
Nope, no good.

345
00:14:26,115 --> 00:14:27,575
Still bleeding
like a stuck pig.

346
00:14:27,616 --> 00:14:28,576
- I can give it a try.

347
00:14:28,617 --> 00:14:29,952
- Dr. Garcia is
a very skilled surgeon.

348
00:14:29,994 --> 00:14:31,620
If she can't get it, we need
to think of something else.

349
00:14:31,662 --> 00:14:32,872
- Like what?

350
00:14:36,333 --> 00:14:37,793
- Hilar flip.

351
00:14:37,835 --> 00:14:39,461
We're going to rotate
the lung 180 degrees,

352
00:14:39,503 --> 00:14:41,088
like putting a kink
in the garden hose.

353
00:14:41,130 --> 00:14:43,799
It'll stop the bleeding.
- That could possibly work.

354
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:47,595
- Or he'll die when you rip
the entire lung off the hilum.

355
00:14:47,636 --> 00:14:50,431
- Gently, very, very gently,
very, very slowly.

356
00:14:50,472 --> 00:14:53,517
[respirator hissing]

357
00:15:06,113 --> 00:15:07,865
- Blood loss is slowing down.
- That's a good sign.

358
00:15:07,948 --> 00:15:09,158
- Hilum looks dry.

359
00:15:09,241 --> 00:15:10,993
- Yeah, but the heart
feels empty.

360
00:15:11,076 --> 00:15:13,537
- Okay, we fixed the leak.
Now let's fill the tank.

361
00:15:13,621 --> 00:15:15,706
[cell phone chimes]

362
00:15:15,789 --> 00:15:17,833
You are very popular today.

363
00:15:17,875 --> 00:15:20,669
- Sorry, I'll turn it off
when I can.

364
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:26,508
- Nine-year-old Kylie Connors--

365
00:15:26,550 --> 00:15:28,677
brought in by
her dad's girlfriend, Gina.

366
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:31,138
Kylie took a tumble
on the stairs this morning.

367
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:34,099
3-centimeter chin lac
and a chipped front incisor.

368
00:15:34,183 --> 00:15:36,018
She's got LET
on a pressure dressing.

369
00:15:36,101 --> 00:15:37,853
Bleeding pretty good
on arrival.

370
00:15:37,937 --> 00:15:40,022
Dermabond should do the trick.

371
00:15:40,105 --> 00:15:42,107
Any questions?

372
00:15:42,191 --> 00:15:44,526
Dr. King?

373
00:15:44,568 --> 00:15:45,945
Mel?

374
00:15:46,028 --> 00:15:48,614
- Oh, um,
I didn't say anything.

375
00:15:51,408 --> 00:15:54,161
- Uh, how you feeling, Kylie?
- Tired.

376
00:15:54,203 --> 00:15:56,914
- We've been here since 5:30.
- Oh, that makes two of us.

377
00:15:56,997 --> 00:15:59,333
But don't worry.
We'll patch you up good as new.

378
00:15:59,375 --> 00:16:01,919
And we will get you out of here
as soon as we can.

379
00:16:02,002 --> 00:16:03,379
Any questions?
- Can you raise your chin?

380
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:05,214
- No, I'm just
her dad's girlfriend,

381
00:16:05,297 --> 00:16:07,257
so I'm new to all this.
- No worries. We got you.

382
00:16:07,341 --> 00:16:08,634
Next.

383
00:16:10,219 --> 00:16:14,056
- 26-year-old Liam Sanders--
took a fall from his bicycle.

384
00:16:14,098 --> 00:16:16,183
No LOC, nonfocal neuro,

385
00:16:16,225 --> 00:16:18,477
lower-leg lac
with no bony tenderness.

386
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:20,312
- Were you wearing
a helmet, Liam?

387
00:16:20,396 --> 00:16:22,231
- No, I was just riding
in my neighborhood.

388
00:16:22,272 --> 00:16:23,983
- You should always
wear a helmet.

389
00:16:24,066 --> 00:16:25,484
- Hey, nice of you to join us.

390
00:16:25,567 --> 00:16:27,653
- Hey.
- Dr. Langdon!

391
00:16:30,072 --> 00:16:32,825
- Sold to Dr. Langdon.

392
00:16:32,908 --> 00:16:35,160
- Candace O'Grady, 82...
[person coughs]

393
00:16:35,244 --> 00:16:39,415
Brought in by her son Richard
for intractable vomiting.

394
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:43,002
- Hardly any hemorrhage
on the right.

395
00:16:43,085 --> 00:16:44,420
- Looks like the flip worked.
- How's the heart, Whitaker?

396
00:16:44,503 --> 00:16:46,422
- Feels full of blood.
First time since we started.

397
00:16:46,463 --> 00:16:50,300
- Hold compressions.

398
00:16:50,384 --> 00:16:52,970
- Quivering bag of worms.
- Resume compressions.

399
00:16:53,053 --> 00:16:55,472
- When the heart quivers
like that, it's obvious V-fib.

400
00:16:55,556 --> 00:16:58,767
- Okay, internal paddles ready.
- Mm-hmm. Charge to...?

401
00:16:58,851 --> 00:17:00,602
- 25 joules.
- 50 joules.

402
00:17:00,644 --> 00:17:02,479
50 gets it right
the first time.

403
00:17:02,563 --> 00:17:04,647
[paddles whirring]
- Charged.

404
00:17:04,732 --> 00:17:05,941
- Okay, Whitaker,
when you're ready.

405
00:17:06,025 --> 00:17:08,068
- Clear.

406
00:17:10,654 --> 00:17:12,781
- That's what normal sinus
looks like.

407
00:17:14,575 --> 00:17:15,617
- Strong carotid.

408
00:17:15,659 --> 00:17:18,746
- Saline lap pads
and up to the OR.

409
00:17:18,787 --> 00:17:21,040
- That's a hell of a way
to start the day.

410
00:17:21,123 --> 00:17:24,877
- Unconventional,
but a decent outcome.

411
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:27,212
- Why don't we split up
for efficiency?

412
00:17:27,296 --> 00:17:29,631
Residents present to either
one of us but not both.

413
00:17:31,133 --> 00:17:33,093
- We can certainly
discuss that.

414
00:17:37,556 --> 00:17:39,475
- Were you feeling weak
or dizzy?

415
00:17:39,516 --> 00:17:40,809
- [sighs]
No.

416
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:42,561
I-I got up to pee,
and I tripped over

417
00:17:42,644 --> 00:17:44,605
a laundry basket that
I couldn't see in the dark.

418
00:17:44,646 --> 00:17:46,148
- Did you hit your head?

419
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:48,067
- Does my chart say
that I hit my head?

420
00:17:48,150 --> 00:17:50,277
- No, we just
want to be thorough

421
00:17:50,319 --> 00:17:52,488
to make sure
you don't miss anything.

422
00:17:52,571 --> 00:17:54,823
- How about we focus
on patching me up?

423
00:17:54,907 --> 00:17:56,492
Ah!

424
00:17:56,533 --> 00:17:58,494
- All right, Mr. Williams,

425
00:17:58,535 --> 00:18:00,287
we're going to check an X-ray
of your wrist

426
00:18:00,329 --> 00:18:02,498
and try to get you out of here
as quickly as possible.

427
00:18:02,539 --> 00:18:05,000
- Great. And let's keep
the bullshit extra charges

428
00:18:05,042 --> 00:18:06,502
to a minimum, huh?

429
00:18:06,543 --> 00:18:08,796
My deductible
is already a bitch.

430
00:18:08,837 --> 00:18:11,298
- [sighs]

431
00:18:11,340 --> 00:18:13,175
Sure.

432
00:18:15,594 --> 00:18:18,639
[Samira speaking Tamil]

433
00:18:34,863 --> 00:18:36,198
Bye.

434
00:18:36,281 --> 00:18:38,700
My mother is trying
to destroy my life.

435
00:18:38,742 --> 00:18:40,869
- Yeah, they seem
to take pleasure in that.

436
00:18:40,911 --> 00:18:42,704
- I committed
to a partnership-track position

437
00:18:42,746 --> 00:18:44,540
next year at a hospital
back in Jersey

438
00:18:44,581 --> 00:18:46,542
so I could be close
and support her.

439
00:18:46,583 --> 00:18:49,545
But now she's getting married
and selling our house

440
00:18:49,586 --> 00:18:52,047
to go on a year-long cruise
around the world.

441
00:18:52,131 --> 00:18:54,675
- Wow.
I mean, that's a--

442
00:18:54,716 --> 00:18:56,593
- Pretty fucking stupid idea,
if you ask me.

443
00:18:56,677 --> 00:18:58,720
She's known this guy
less than a year.

444
00:18:58,762 --> 00:19:02,057
What if he's a player?
What if she disappears at sea?

445
00:19:02,141 --> 00:19:03,851
- I mean,
what if they're in love?

446
00:19:03,892 --> 00:19:05,519
- Lust is more like it.

447
00:19:05,561 --> 00:19:08,605
- That is even better.

448
00:19:08,689 --> 00:19:11,900
Man, it has been a while,

449
00:19:11,984 --> 00:19:15,362
and I'm solo this weekend.

450
00:19:15,404 --> 00:19:17,656
I need to get laid.

451
00:19:21,535 --> 00:19:23,579
It's a little much
before 8:00 a.m., right?

452
00:19:23,662 --> 00:19:25,497
- [scoffs]
Yeah.

453
00:19:26,999 --> 00:19:28,792
Or ever.

454
00:19:34,256 --> 00:19:35,757
- How's it going, Esme?

455
00:19:35,799 --> 00:19:38,051
- Oh, just another day
in paradise.

456
00:19:51,815 --> 00:19:53,859
[device whirs]

457
00:19:55,277 --> 00:19:57,946
- Heard you're going on a trip.

458
00:19:57,988 --> 00:20:00,157
- Taking a sabbatical.
Leave tonight.

459
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:01,992
- Where you going?

460
00:20:02,075 --> 00:20:05,829
- Um, Head-Smashed-In
Buffalo Jump.

461
00:20:05,913 --> 00:20:08,624
- Captain Crunch
flying squirrel socks.

462
00:20:08,707 --> 00:20:11,960
What, are we talking in code,
or are you having a stroke?

463
00:20:12,002 --> 00:20:15,422
- It's an Aboriginal UNESCO
Heritage Site in Alberta.

464
00:20:15,464 --> 00:20:17,424
I've never seen the Badlands.

465
00:20:17,466 --> 00:20:20,636
- What is this, like, uh,
some sort of spirit quest?

466
00:20:20,677 --> 00:20:23,931
- Uh, yeah,
something like that.

467
00:20:23,972 --> 00:20:26,516
- Look, I, uh--
I guess we should talk.

468
00:20:26,600 --> 00:20:28,894
Got a lot of things I need to--
- At some point, not right now.

469
00:20:28,977 --> 00:20:30,562
Right now
I got to hit the head,

470
00:20:30,646 --> 00:20:32,564
and I would like you to go
help Donnie out in Triage.

471
00:20:32,648 --> 00:20:33,941
- Triage?

472
00:20:33,982 --> 00:20:35,484
N-no.

473
00:20:35,525 --> 00:20:37,152
No, I already picked up
a patient in North 2,

474
00:20:37,236 --> 00:20:38,612
a bicycle fall leg lac,
so maybe--

475
00:20:38,654 --> 00:20:39,738
- Pretty sure
we can cover for you.

476
00:20:39,821 --> 00:20:42,991
We've been doing it for months.

477
00:20:43,075 --> 00:20:44,618
- Right.

478
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:47,162
No, and I--and I am--
I am very, uh--

479
00:20:47,246 --> 00:20:48,914
both very sorry
and appreciative of that.

480
00:20:48,997 --> 00:20:50,415
I just would hate to think

481
00:20:50,499 --> 00:20:53,085
that this is some kind
of a punishment for...

482
00:20:53,168 --> 00:20:55,003
but, uh, yeah.
Yeah. No, for sure.

483
00:20:55,087 --> 00:20:58,674
We'll--we'll talk later,
before you go.

484
00:20:58,715 --> 00:21:01,134
- Like I said, not right now.

485
00:21:01,176 --> 00:21:03,512
Donnie just passed
his NP certification.

486
00:21:03,553 --> 00:21:05,472
He can help if you get in
over your head.

487
00:21:08,934 --> 00:21:10,852
Hey, Mel...

488
00:21:10,894 --> 00:21:13,689
can you take over
a bike fall leg lac in North 2

489
00:21:13,772 --> 00:21:15,565
for Langdon
while he's out in Triage?

490
00:21:15,649 --> 00:21:17,359
- Um, you know, I-I can work
in Triage if you want--

491
00:21:17,442 --> 00:21:19,236
- No, I want to keep Langdon
out in Triage,

492
00:21:19,319 --> 00:21:20,988
and I want to keep you
back here.

493
00:21:21,029 --> 00:21:23,490
You okay with that?
- Sure.

494
00:21:23,532 --> 00:21:25,534
- Great.
- Absolutely.

495
00:21:31,540 --> 00:21:33,208
- So...

496
00:21:33,250 --> 00:21:35,460
who should I pick up?

497
00:21:35,544 --> 00:21:37,504
- We're about to get
the morning rush.

498
00:21:37,546 --> 00:21:38,714
- What do you mean?

499
00:21:38,755 --> 00:21:40,215
- Every morning
around this time,

500
00:21:40,257 --> 00:21:42,634
the nursing homes
and assisted-living facilities

501
00:21:42,718 --> 00:21:45,387
do their morning bed checks.
- Uh-huh.

502
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:50,684
It looks pretty quiet to me.

503
00:21:53,937 --> 00:21:56,064
- Huh.

504
00:21:56,148 --> 00:21:57,816
- Incoming.

505
00:21:57,899 --> 00:21:59,526
- Oh, like I said.

506
00:21:59,568 --> 00:22:01,069
Come on.

507
00:22:01,111 --> 00:22:02,571
- Ethan Bostick, 79--

508
00:22:02,654 --> 00:22:04,698
found altered this morning
at assisted living.

509
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:06,033
No history of dementia.

510
00:22:06,074 --> 00:22:07,909
Has a POLST.
- What's a POLST?

511
00:22:07,951 --> 00:22:10,537
- Physician Orders
for Life-Sustaining Treatment--

512
00:22:10,579 --> 00:22:12,956
no CPR, no intubation.

513
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,083
- Then there's--
there's nothing for us to do.

514
00:22:15,125 --> 00:22:16,918
- South 15 is open.

515
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:19,921
Uh, well, "do not resuscitate"
does not mean "do not treat."

516
00:22:19,963 --> 00:22:23,759
He may perk up with fluids,
IV antibiotics.

517
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:26,803
- [speaking Korean]

518
00:22:28,597 --> 00:22:29,806
- What's the good word, Crash?

519
00:22:29,890 --> 00:22:32,184
You save
that ventriloquist dummy?

520
00:22:32,267 --> 00:22:33,935
- Yeah.
- Really?

521
00:22:33,977 --> 00:22:36,355
- No.
- [laughs]

522
00:22:36,438 --> 00:22:38,357
Wait, tell me
you're not actually charting

523
00:22:38,440 --> 00:22:40,567
about losing a mannequin.
- It's part of the exercise.

524
00:22:40,609 --> 00:22:42,361
Dr. Al-Hashimi
asked us to do it.

525
00:22:42,444 --> 00:22:44,363
- Who's his next of kin--
Barbie?

526
00:22:44,446 --> 00:22:45,947
[chuckles]

527
00:22:45,989 --> 00:22:49,951
What's your, uh, take
on the new attending?

528
00:22:49,993 --> 00:22:51,661
- Too soon to tell.

529
00:22:53,622 --> 00:22:54,873
You?
- [sighs]

530
00:22:54,956 --> 00:22:56,124
She's already bugging me

531
00:22:56,208 --> 00:22:57,793
about being behind
on my charting.

532
00:22:57,834 --> 00:22:59,461
Now I know why everyone says

533
00:22:59,503 --> 00:23:01,713
being a second-year resident
sucks--because it does.

534
00:23:01,797 --> 00:23:03,507
- I must've slept through
the part of med school

535
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:06,343
where they tell you 90% of
your time will be spent typing.

536
00:23:06,426 --> 00:23:07,969
- Which is just one more reason

537
00:23:08,011 --> 00:23:10,138
surgery is
a better lifestyle choice.

538
00:23:10,222 --> 00:23:13,141
- Hey, Dr. Shamsi.
Dr. Trinity Santos.

539
00:23:13,225 --> 00:23:15,352
- Ah, yes, the ER intern
who's interested

540
00:23:15,435 --> 00:23:17,354
in my surgery program.
- That's me.

541
00:23:17,437 --> 00:23:19,981
But I'm an R2 now and thinking
of double boarding

542
00:23:20,023 --> 00:23:21,817
in surgery
and emergency medicine,

543
00:23:21,858 --> 00:23:24,319
which I know would sound
ridiculously ambitious

544
00:23:24,361 --> 00:23:26,655
if I didn't have the talent
to back it up.

545
00:23:26,696 --> 00:23:28,281
- And the required attitude.

546
00:23:29,908 --> 00:23:31,618
Are you here to see a patient?

547
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:33,370
- I was. I did.

548
00:23:33,453 --> 00:23:34,788
And now I'm here to learn

549
00:23:34,830 --> 00:23:37,416
why you aren't returning
my calls or texts.

550
00:23:37,499 --> 00:23:39,000
- Okay, I'm out.

551
00:23:39,042 --> 00:23:41,086
Nice seeing you again,
Dr. Shamsi.

552
00:23:41,169 --> 00:23:42,671
- As soon as I get
a little free time,

553
00:23:42,712 --> 00:23:44,756
I will make plans for all of us
to do something together.

554
00:23:44,840 --> 00:23:46,633
- What about Tuesday?
- I'm working.

555
00:23:46,675 --> 00:23:48,510
- On your birthday?

556
00:23:48,552 --> 00:23:50,262
- I will call you
and Dad tomorrow,

557
00:23:50,345 --> 00:23:52,013
and we'll come up with something
to do together, I promise.

558
00:23:52,055 --> 00:23:54,015
But right now I actually have

559
00:23:54,057 --> 00:23:56,309
a bunch
of night-shift hand-offs.

560
00:23:58,687 --> 00:24:00,188
- [clears throat]

561
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:07,070
- We have schedules.
You have chaos.

562
00:24:07,154 --> 00:24:09,197
You know she's wasting
her talents down here.

563
00:24:09,281 --> 00:24:12,200
- Victoria is a big girl.
She can make her own decisions.

564
00:24:12,284 --> 00:24:15,829
- She's also a young girl who's
still very impressionable.

565
00:24:15,871 --> 00:24:18,874
And you can be very...
- "Pressionable"?

566
00:24:18,915 --> 00:24:21,418
[cell phone ringing]

567
00:24:21,501 --> 00:24:22,836
- Yes?

568
00:24:22,878 --> 00:24:24,212
Who?

569
00:24:24,254 --> 00:24:25,672
Who?

570
00:24:25,714 --> 00:24:28,425
Oh, for God's sake.
I'll be right there.

571
00:24:28,508 --> 00:24:30,927
We need to talk.

572
00:24:31,011 --> 00:24:33,346
- [whispering]
How about same time tomorrow?

573
00:24:35,932 --> 00:24:37,726
- Next Tuesday's your birthday?

574
00:24:37,767 --> 00:24:39,978
- No.

575
00:24:40,061 --> 00:24:42,230
Yes.
- Your 21st birthday?

576
00:24:42,314 --> 00:24:43,690
- Please don't
say anything, okay?

577
00:24:43,732 --> 00:24:45,066
I-I get a little anxious
about--

578
00:24:45,108 --> 00:24:46,568
- Dr. Santos, little girl
with the chin lac

579
00:24:46,610 --> 00:24:48,069
is getting into a gown.

580
00:24:48,111 --> 00:24:50,697
[whispering]
Don't want anyone to know what?

581
00:24:50,739 --> 00:24:52,991
- Nothing.

582
00:24:53,074 --> 00:24:54,910
- Have a good day.
- You too.

583
00:24:54,993 --> 00:24:56,578
- [speaking Tagalog]

584
00:24:56,661 --> 00:24:57,996
- [normal voice]
Oh.

585
00:24:59,414 --> 00:25:02,083
- Hey, Duke, I'm just calling
to remind you

586
00:25:02,125 --> 00:25:05,086
that this is my last shift
for a while.

587
00:25:05,128 --> 00:25:07,589
So I need you to come in
before 3:00

588
00:25:07,672 --> 00:25:11,092
in case we need
to run some tests.

589
00:25:11,134 --> 00:25:13,803
Okay?
We had a deal, brother.

590
00:25:15,847 --> 00:25:17,933
Oh. Hello.
- Hi.

591
00:25:17,974 --> 00:25:20,101
I know you're crazy busy.
- Always.

592
00:25:20,143 --> 00:25:22,145
- But I figured we should
really go over a few things

593
00:25:22,229 --> 00:25:23,730
before you're gone.
- Sure.

594
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:25,774
Where would you like to start?

595
00:25:25,857 --> 00:25:27,484
- Well, one little thing
that I think

596
00:25:27,567 --> 00:25:30,111
would have a big impact
with staff and patients alike

597
00:25:30,153 --> 00:25:31,947
is if we launched a campaign

598
00:25:31,988 --> 00:25:35,116
to eradicate referring
to this wonderful department

599
00:25:35,158 --> 00:25:37,744
as the Pitt.
- Really?

600
00:25:37,786 --> 00:25:39,746
- I know you think
it's silly and petty.

601
00:25:39,788 --> 00:25:41,748
- No, I think it's
just kind of endearing,

602
00:25:41,790 --> 00:25:45,627
pretty damn accurate.
- I would argue the opposite.

603
00:25:45,669 --> 00:25:48,129
I think subconsciously
it affects those who work here.

604
00:25:48,171 --> 00:25:49,756
It also lowers expectations,

605
00:25:49,798 --> 00:25:52,133
which in turn lowers
patient-satisfaction scores.

606
00:25:52,175 --> 00:25:53,969
- You have been talking
to Gloria.

607
00:25:54,010 --> 00:25:57,389
- Yes, and she agrees with me.
- Don't doubt that for a moment.

608
00:25:57,472 --> 00:25:59,266
- You have to see the man
over there right now!

609
00:25:59,307 --> 00:26:01,810
- We see people in order
of severity, ma'am.

610
00:26:01,893 --> 00:26:05,230
- [speaking Ukrainian]

611
00:26:05,313 --> 00:26:06,731
- I don't know
what you're saying.

612
00:26:06,815 --> 00:26:09,067
- I can taste the shit
in the back of my throat.

613
00:26:09,150 --> 00:26:10,318
- Actually, the lady's right.

614
00:26:10,402 --> 00:26:11,987
He's going to make
all of us sick.

615
00:26:12,028 --> 00:26:13,029
- Yes, please.

616
00:26:13,113 --> 00:26:15,240
[indistinct chatter]

617
00:26:17,867 --> 00:26:19,828
- [coughing]

618
00:26:19,911 --> 00:26:21,454
- All right.
[sighs]

619
00:26:21,496 --> 00:26:22,998
We'll get him back.

620
00:26:23,039 --> 00:26:25,375
Tell Dana
we're bringing the funk.

621
00:26:25,458 --> 00:26:27,502
- Oh, my God.
- Whew!

622
00:26:28,336 --> 00:26:30,797
- So when did your eye start
bothering you, Ms. Mathews?

623
00:26:30,839 --> 00:26:32,841
- Sister Grace, please.

624
00:26:32,882 --> 00:26:34,843
I'm only married to God.

625
00:26:34,926 --> 00:26:36,553
- Of course, I'm sorry.

626
00:26:36,636 --> 00:26:40,181
- A few days ago.
I thought it was pink eye

627
00:26:40,223 --> 00:26:42,350
or maybe I scratched it
while gardening.

628
00:26:42,434 --> 00:26:44,603
- It's a very bad case
of conjunctivitis.

629
00:26:44,686 --> 00:26:46,354
We should send a sample
to the lab

630
00:26:46,438 --> 00:26:47,856
to direct our treatment.

631
00:26:47,897 --> 00:26:49,774
Look up for me.
Thank you.

632
00:26:53,028 --> 00:26:55,822
Okay, I'll put a numbing drop
in your eye

633
00:26:55,864 --> 00:26:58,908
and check for a scratch
with a special lamp.

634
00:27:01,870 --> 00:27:03,830
And I'll get you
to look up for me again.

635
00:27:03,872 --> 00:27:05,624
Thank you.

636
00:27:05,707 --> 00:27:08,126
- Have you ever considered
serving God?

637
00:27:08,209 --> 00:27:09,294
- Which one?

638
00:27:09,377 --> 00:27:11,630
- Excuse me?

639
00:27:11,713 --> 00:27:13,381
- My family is Hindu,

640
00:27:13,465 --> 00:27:16,217
so we have, like,
33 different kinds of deities.

641
00:27:16,259 --> 00:27:18,428
- Oh, we have the one.

642
00:27:18,511 --> 00:27:21,514
- Some people
only need one, right?

643
00:27:21,556 --> 00:27:23,016
Okay.

644
00:27:23,058 --> 00:27:25,101
[camera shutter clicks]

645
00:27:25,185 --> 00:27:28,021
- Most important rule
around here--be smart, be safe.

646
00:27:28,063 --> 00:27:30,065
I can't warn you enough
about protecting yourself

647
00:27:30,106 --> 00:27:31,441
and your fellow nurses.

648
00:27:31,524 --> 00:27:33,902
We use the STAMP acronym
to assess patients.

649
00:27:33,985 --> 00:27:35,362
You know it?

650
00:27:35,403 --> 00:27:37,906
- Uh, S is for staring
or refusal to make eye contact.

651
00:27:37,947 --> 00:27:39,574
- If they're staring,
they're scaring.

652
00:27:39,616 --> 00:27:41,076
T?
- Tone of voice.

653
00:27:41,159 --> 00:27:43,453
- Often the threat level
rises with their voice.

654
00:27:43,536 --> 00:27:44,913
- A is for anxiety.

655
00:27:44,996 --> 00:27:46,581
- You can also think
"aggression."

656
00:27:46,665 --> 00:27:47,999
- M is mumbling.

657
00:27:48,083 --> 00:27:49,250
- Which, if you
listen closely enough,

658
00:27:49,292 --> 00:27:50,919
is often them saying
what they're thinking of doing

659
00:27:50,960 --> 00:27:52,587
as they talk themselves
up to it.

660
00:27:52,629 --> 00:27:54,089
And P?
- Pacing.

661
00:27:54,172 --> 00:27:55,507
- Good.

662
00:27:55,590 --> 00:27:57,425
We also have a safe word--
"hula hoop."

663
00:27:57,509 --> 00:27:59,260
I guess technically
it's two words, but whatever.

664
00:27:59,302 --> 00:28:01,262
If there's a threat,
say "hula hoop,"

665
00:28:01,304 --> 00:28:04,683
and we'll all respond.
[cell phone ringing]

666
00:28:04,766 --> 00:28:06,267
Yeah?

667
00:28:06,309 --> 00:28:07,727
Lovely.

668
00:28:07,769 --> 00:28:11,898
Okay, I'll be right there
with reinforcements.

669
00:28:11,940 --> 00:28:14,776
Duty calls.
We're headed for Triage.

670
00:28:17,904 --> 00:28:20,448
[indistinct chatter]

671
00:28:20,532 --> 00:28:22,450
- We're going to get
your cast off--

672
00:28:22,492 --> 00:28:25,537
[low humming]

673
00:28:41,553 --> 00:28:44,180
- The antibiotics
should clear everything up,

674
00:28:44,264 --> 00:28:47,142
but you should try to stay
off it for a few days.

675
00:28:47,183 --> 00:28:50,478
- Uh, I have to work.
I'm late as it is.

676
00:28:50,562 --> 00:28:54,482
- Well, um, take breaks
and elevate when you can.

677
00:28:54,566 --> 00:28:56,317
Tylenol and Advil for the pain.

678
00:28:56,401 --> 00:28:59,112
If the pain persists or feel
like it's getting worse,

679
00:28:59,154 --> 00:29:00,321
come on back.

680
00:29:00,363 --> 00:29:02,657
We'll get you fixed up.
- Thanks.

681
00:29:02,699 --> 00:29:05,660
- They said give this
to the doctor.

682
00:29:05,744 --> 00:29:08,747
- What the hell is it?
- Patient passport.

683
00:29:08,830 --> 00:29:10,749
Lets patients know
what they're waiting for

684
00:29:10,832 --> 00:29:12,250
and how long it'll take.

685
00:29:12,333 --> 00:29:14,502
You check off if he needs labs,
X-rays, CT.

686
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:16,504
- None of the above.
When did we start this?

687
00:29:16,546 --> 00:29:18,506
- Today, the new attending.

688
00:29:18,590 --> 00:29:20,300
- [scoffs]

689
00:29:20,341 --> 00:29:22,635
What's up
with your tooth, Louie?

690
00:29:22,677 --> 00:29:25,346
- It's been going on
for a while,

691
00:29:25,388 --> 00:29:27,348
but it got bad last night.

692
00:29:27,390 --> 00:29:29,976
[groans]
Lower-left side.

693
00:29:30,018 --> 00:29:32,145
- Okay.

694
00:29:32,187 --> 00:29:35,565
- [groaning in pain]
- Oh, yeah.

695
00:29:35,648 --> 00:29:37,484
It could be an infection
at the root.

696
00:29:37,525 --> 00:29:40,653
- [groans]
- We can drain it, but then...

697
00:29:40,695 --> 00:29:42,822
you're, uh--you're breathing
kind of fast.

698
00:29:42,864 --> 00:29:44,699
- [exhales sharply]
Yeah, I've been--

699
00:29:44,783 --> 00:29:48,203
well, I've been,
uh, walking more,

700
00:29:48,244 --> 00:29:49,871
trying to get in shape.

701
00:29:49,913 --> 00:29:52,707
But sometimes I get
a little short of breath.

702
00:29:52,749 --> 00:29:55,710
- Yeah, you need to walk past
the liquor store, not into it.

703
00:29:55,794 --> 00:29:58,379
Only shape you're getting in
is for a liver transplant.

704
00:29:58,421 --> 00:30:01,382
- I don't need a new liver.
Just get rid of this toothache.

705
00:30:01,424 --> 00:30:02,801
- Deep breath.

706
00:30:02,884 --> 00:30:06,554
- [breathes deeply]

707
00:30:06,638 --> 00:30:08,223
- One more.

708
00:30:08,264 --> 00:30:10,809
- Is my heart still ticking?
- Like a jackrabbit.

709
00:30:10,892 --> 00:30:14,062
How long you been sitting down?
- Oh, just for a few minutes.

710
00:30:14,103 --> 00:30:15,563
Not that long.

711
00:30:15,647 --> 00:30:17,482
- Um, Louie...

712
00:30:19,275 --> 00:30:21,486
There's something
I need to tell you.

713
00:30:23,822 --> 00:30:27,033
Last time I saw you,
about ten months ago,

714
00:30:27,075 --> 00:30:31,788
I treated you, and I took
some of your medicine.

715
00:30:31,871 --> 00:30:34,916
- I'm not sure I follow.

716
00:30:34,958 --> 00:30:37,585
- I have an addiction
to benzos,

717
00:30:37,627 --> 00:30:41,256
and I helped myself to some
of the Librium I prescribed you

718
00:30:41,297 --> 00:30:42,882
for your withdrawal.

719
00:30:42,924 --> 00:30:44,843
- You stole my pills?

720
00:30:46,553 --> 00:30:47,971
- Yeah.

721
00:30:48,054 --> 00:30:50,473
Yes, um...

722
00:30:50,557 --> 00:30:53,101
I'm sorry.
It was, uh...

723
00:30:53,184 --> 00:30:56,062
it was not only wrong
and utterly unprofessional,

724
00:30:56,104 --> 00:30:58,773
but it was a betrayal
of my Hippocratic Oath,

725
00:30:58,857 --> 00:31:02,068
and it was a fucking crime.

726
00:31:02,110 --> 00:31:06,531
That's where I've been--
rehab and counseling.

727
00:31:08,324 --> 00:31:10,869
- Huh.
- I'm sorry.

728
00:31:10,952 --> 00:31:13,788
I-I understand if you'd prefer
a different doctor.

729
00:31:13,830 --> 00:31:17,208
- Hey, Doc,
we have a situation.

730
00:31:17,292 --> 00:31:19,627
- Excuse me.

731
00:31:19,711 --> 00:31:21,087
What kind of situation?

732
00:31:21,129 --> 00:31:23,631
- Someone left a little baby
in the restroom.

733
00:31:23,715 --> 00:31:26,050
[baby crying]

734
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:33,766
- Okay, let's think

735
00:31:33,808 --> 00:31:36,644
about reversible causes
of confusion in the elderly.

736
00:31:36,728 --> 00:31:40,315
- Uh, hypo or hypernatremia,
infection, but he's afebrile,

737
00:31:40,356 --> 00:31:42,191
good O2 sats,
and urine dip is negative.

738
00:31:42,275 --> 00:31:46,321
- Yeah, great.
Joy, anything to add?

739
00:31:46,362 --> 00:31:48,865
- Nah.
[alarm blaring]

740
00:31:48,948 --> 00:31:51,409
- V-tach. Can I--
- Yeah.

741
00:31:53,161 --> 00:31:54,996
- No pulse.
Should we call for a code blue?

742
00:31:55,079 --> 00:31:59,000
- No CPR, no shock, we honor
his POLST and his wishes.

743
00:31:59,083 --> 00:32:02,629
- So what does that leave us?

744
00:32:02,670 --> 00:32:05,256
- Making sure he's comfortable.

745
00:32:05,340 --> 00:32:06,799
- I'll suction.

746
00:32:06,841 --> 00:32:09,010
- Altered mental
in the elderly can also be

747
00:32:09,052 --> 00:32:13,473
toxicity from anticholinergics,
sedative-hypnotics, opioids.

748
00:32:14,933 --> 00:32:17,143
A-am I missing something?

749
00:32:17,185 --> 00:32:19,187
- Social skills?

750
00:32:19,270 --> 00:32:21,522
- [chuckles]
- What?

751
00:32:21,564 --> 00:32:24,025
[blaring continues]

752
00:32:24,108 --> 00:32:25,360
- He looks so peaceful.

753
00:32:25,443 --> 00:32:26,569
- Shouldn't we go find
a new patient?

754
00:32:26,653 --> 00:32:29,197
- No, stay here.

755
00:32:29,280 --> 00:32:31,032
[flatline]

756
00:32:31,074 --> 00:32:33,117
- Asystole.

757
00:32:42,043 --> 00:32:44,253
- Quick and painless.

758
00:32:44,337 --> 00:32:46,047
We should all be so lucky.

759
00:32:46,130 --> 00:32:48,174
- I'll try to contact
some family.

760
00:32:48,216 --> 00:32:49,717
[flatline stops]

761
00:32:49,801 --> 00:32:53,262
- Yeah, one of the things
we like to do here...

762
00:32:56,140 --> 00:32:58,142
When we have the time...

763
00:32:58,226 --> 00:33:00,979
is to take a moment of silence
when we lose a patient

764
00:33:01,062 --> 00:33:03,398
to respect their humanity.

765
00:33:03,439 --> 00:33:07,527
He was someone's son,
perhaps a father himself,

766
00:33:07,568 --> 00:33:10,071
a brother or a friend.

767
00:33:12,824 --> 00:33:16,244
Oh, uh, make sure
your phones are on silent.

768
00:33:20,415 --> 00:33:23,001
Great, great, great.
Okay. Uh...

769
00:33:23,084 --> 00:33:26,421
- Is anyone missing a baby?

770
00:33:26,462 --> 00:33:30,758
Did anyone leave their child
in the restroom?

771
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:34,262
- Do you, uh, remember seeing
a woman enter with a baby

772
00:33:34,303 --> 00:33:36,097
but leave without one?

773
00:33:36,180 --> 00:33:38,433
- No, sorry.

774
00:33:38,474 --> 00:33:40,435
Might be on the security tapes.

775
00:33:40,476 --> 00:33:42,645
I'll have Ahmad check it out.
- Please.

776
00:33:42,729 --> 00:33:43,813
- Yeah.

777
00:33:43,896 --> 00:33:46,149
- Harlow Graham to window one.

778
00:33:46,232 --> 00:33:49,485
Harlow Graham to window one.

779
00:33:49,569 --> 00:33:53,531
Harlow Graham to window one,
please.

780
00:33:58,327 --> 00:34:00,038
- Donnie Donahue,
nurse practitioner.

781
00:34:00,121 --> 00:34:01,456
This is Emma, new grad nurse.

782
00:34:01,497 --> 00:34:02,957
- Hey.
- Nice to meet you.

783
00:34:03,041 --> 00:34:04,792
- What's going on?
- Somebody dumped their kid.

784
00:34:04,876 --> 00:34:07,462
- Maybe Mom just stepped out
for a smoke or something.

785
00:34:07,503 --> 00:34:09,255
What?
I'm being helpful.

786
00:34:09,297 --> 00:34:10,797
Sometimes they just need
a little break,

787
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:13,259
and then they come back.

788
00:34:13,301 --> 00:34:14,886
At least they didn't leave her
in a suitcase

789
00:34:14,969 --> 00:34:17,263
at the bus station.
- Does that happen?

790
00:34:17,304 --> 00:34:19,139
- If that's a true story,
I don't want to know about it.

791
00:34:19,181 --> 00:34:21,266
- It ain't the worst I've seen.
- Please, don't tell me.

792
00:34:21,309 --> 00:34:23,728
I'm a new dad, remember?
- Okay.

793
00:34:26,063 --> 00:34:29,108
[baby crying]

794
00:34:30,693 --> 00:34:32,652
There you go.
I'll get Robby.

795
00:34:32,695 --> 00:34:34,322
- Should I come with?
- No, stay here.

796
00:34:34,405 --> 00:34:36,699
Donnie, Jesse,
teach her something.

797
00:34:39,409 --> 00:34:43,121
- You know how to check
a rectal temp on an infant?

798
00:34:43,164 --> 00:34:44,706
- Uh...

799
00:34:44,791 --> 00:34:46,708
theoretically.

800
00:34:52,215 --> 00:34:55,009
- Mr. Williams, how's the pain?

801
00:34:55,092 --> 00:34:56,677
- Nothing I can't manage

802
00:34:56,719 --> 00:34:58,679
with some Vicodin
and a little scotch.

803
00:34:58,721 --> 00:35:01,265
- [chuckles] Well, it's best
not to mix the two.

804
00:35:01,349 --> 00:35:03,601
James here is going to take you
to get an X-ray of your wrist.

805
00:35:03,684 --> 00:35:05,520
- I'm going to be fine.

806
00:35:05,561 --> 00:35:07,980
- Well, it'll tell us
if you need a cast.

807
00:35:08,022 --> 00:35:09,649
- Oh, I doubt it.

808
00:35:09,690 --> 00:35:11,859
I should probably go home,
keep some ice on it.

809
00:35:11,943 --> 00:35:14,695
I know you guys
always need the beds.

810
00:35:14,737 --> 00:35:17,031
- Well, uh,
it'll only take a minute,

811
00:35:17,115 --> 00:35:18,783
and then we'll know for sure.

812
00:35:18,866 --> 00:35:20,868
- Yeah, I-I really don't think
it's necessary.

813
00:35:20,952 --> 00:35:23,496
And I have a busy day,
but I appreciate your concern.

814
00:35:26,749 --> 00:35:29,210
- I'd really like to observe you
for a little bit,

815
00:35:29,293 --> 00:35:31,838
in case you did hit your head.

816
00:35:31,879 --> 00:35:33,381
- My head?

817
00:35:33,464 --> 00:35:35,883
I hit my schnoz on my arm,
I think, nothing more.

818
00:35:35,925 --> 00:35:38,177
- You think?
You don't remember?

819
00:35:38,219 --> 00:35:39,595
- No.

820
00:35:39,679 --> 00:35:41,430
Or, yes.

821
00:35:41,514 --> 00:35:44,517
I'm going to be fine, really.

822
00:35:44,559 --> 00:35:46,811
But thank you for your concern.

823
00:35:50,523 --> 00:35:52,233
- Okay.

824
00:35:52,316 --> 00:35:54,569
- [shouts in pain]

825
00:35:54,610 --> 00:35:58,239
- How about we take that X-ray
just to be safe?

826
00:35:58,281 --> 00:36:00,741
- Yeah. Yeah, okay.
- Yeah?

827
00:36:00,783 --> 00:36:02,910
Okay.
- Okay, good idea.

828
00:36:02,994 --> 00:36:04,871
- [sighs]

829
00:36:04,912 --> 00:36:06,914
Somebody found a baby?
- Yeah.

830
00:36:06,998 --> 00:36:09,333
Not the first time
it's happened.

831
00:36:09,417 --> 00:36:11,210
So, about those pills I took--

832
00:36:11,252 --> 00:36:14,255
- Oh, don't worry
about it, Doc.

833
00:36:14,297 --> 00:36:17,592
If the pills helped you, great.

834
00:36:17,675 --> 00:36:19,844
I never really liked
taking 'em.

835
00:36:19,927 --> 00:36:21,762
[both chuckle]

836
00:36:21,846 --> 00:36:23,389
- I appreciate that, Louie.

837
00:36:23,431 --> 00:36:25,391
That's not really
the point, but...

838
00:36:25,433 --> 00:36:27,768
I need to lift up
your shirt for a second.

839
00:36:27,852 --> 00:36:30,897
- [groans, sighs]

840
00:36:33,816 --> 00:36:36,110
- How long has your belly
been hard like that?

841
00:36:36,194 --> 00:36:37,612
- I don't know.

842
00:36:37,653 --> 00:36:39,739
I figured I could wait
another week.

843
00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:41,782
- You need, uh, some lab tests

844
00:36:41,824 --> 00:36:44,619
and a procedure to drain off
some of this fluid.

845
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:46,621
- Yeah, I know the drill.

846
00:36:46,662 --> 00:36:48,956
I've been tapped
a few times before.

847
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:52,168
- Three times
in the last six months.

848
00:36:52,251 --> 00:36:54,962
You need to give your liver
a break, Louie.

849
00:36:55,046 --> 00:36:56,464
- Yeah, I know.

850
00:37:01,052 --> 00:37:02,970
- I just heard somebody
dumped a baby

851
00:37:03,054 --> 00:37:04,430
in the waiting-room bathroom.

852
00:37:04,472 --> 00:37:07,642
How sad is that?
- Huh?

853
00:37:07,683 --> 00:37:10,728
- What's up with you today,
Mel...ancholia?

854
00:37:10,811 --> 00:37:13,147
- What makes you think
there's something up with me?

855
00:37:13,231 --> 00:37:14,982
- You're moping
around here all day

856
00:37:15,024 --> 00:37:17,443
like somebody gunned down
your favorite K-Pop band.

857
00:37:17,485 --> 00:37:19,445
- I don't really have
a favorite K-Pop band.

858
00:37:19,487 --> 00:37:21,280
It's not that I don't like
the genre.

859
00:37:21,322 --> 00:37:22,990
It's just kinda--
- Okay, forget K-Pop.

860
00:37:23,074 --> 00:37:24,659
What's going on with you?

861
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:25,993
You usually ride in here

862
00:37:26,077 --> 00:37:27,828
on your unicorn
tossing wildflowers.

863
00:37:27,870 --> 00:37:30,498
- "Unicorn"?
- [sighs] Never mind.

864
00:37:30,581 --> 00:37:33,000
Is everything okay
with your sister?

865
00:37:33,084 --> 00:37:34,752
- Oh, yeah, she's great.

866
00:37:34,835 --> 00:37:36,837
It's just this...

867
00:37:36,879 --> 00:37:38,506
[stammers]

868
00:37:38,547 --> 00:37:40,675
- Spill, girl.

869
00:37:40,716 --> 00:37:42,301
- Um...

870
00:37:42,343 --> 00:37:45,680
well, a-a couple months ago,
I got named in a...

871
00:37:45,721 --> 00:37:47,848
[whispering]
Malpractice lawsuit.

872
00:37:47,890 --> 00:37:51,686
And so, um, today
is the--the deposition.

873
00:37:51,769 --> 00:37:53,396
- That's it?

874
00:37:53,479 --> 00:37:56,565
Every emergency-medicine doc
gets sued sooner or later.

875
00:37:56,649 --> 00:37:59,026
Seriously, don't sweat it.
You're still a resident.

876
00:37:59,068 --> 00:38:00,695
We're protected
by the hospital.

877
00:38:00,736 --> 00:38:04,532
You could cut somebody's head
off and get away with it.

878
00:38:04,573 --> 00:38:06,909
But, you know, like, don't.

879
00:38:06,993 --> 00:38:09,370
You don't want to become
known as the one

880
00:38:09,453 --> 00:38:11,998
who put Mel in mel-practice.

881
00:38:12,039 --> 00:38:14,792
- Hey, Santos, our little girl,
Kylie, has been

882
00:38:14,875 --> 00:38:18,045
in the West Penn Emergency
Department a couple times.

883
00:38:18,129 --> 00:38:20,715
- I'm not saying this
is necessarily the case here,

884
00:38:20,756 --> 00:38:23,509
but recent studies
have linked bullying behavior

885
00:38:23,551 --> 00:38:26,387
to different
personality disorders.

886
00:38:26,470 --> 00:38:29,056
On the other hand, though,
I-I am known to be a bit

887
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:30,558
overly sensitive sometimes--

888
00:38:30,599 --> 00:38:31,726
- Mel.
- Yes?

889
00:38:31,809 --> 00:38:33,060
- You're talking
to yourself again.

890
00:38:33,144 --> 00:38:34,895
You need something?
- Uh, yeah, a good lawyer.

891
00:38:34,979 --> 00:38:36,731
[chuckles]
I don't know. I'm kidding.

892
00:38:36,772 --> 00:38:38,065
I'm--

893
00:38:39,984 --> 00:38:42,111
- Yo, Robinavitch, somebody
ditched a bundle of joy

894
00:38:42,194 --> 00:38:43,404
in chairs.

895
00:38:43,487 --> 00:38:44,905
- How old?
- Couple months maybe.

896
00:38:44,947 --> 00:38:46,282
Donahue's got her in Pedes.

897
00:38:46,365 --> 00:38:48,743
- No one saw who left her?
- Apparently not.

898
00:38:48,826 --> 00:38:50,536
- That's so sad.
- Tell me about it.

899
00:38:50,578 --> 00:38:52,079
Who throws away
a perfectly good baby?

900
00:38:52,121 --> 00:38:53,247
- [laughs]

901
00:38:53,289 --> 00:38:54,582
- Make a lane!

902
00:38:54,665 --> 00:38:57,084
- Ah, sweet baby Jesus,
Antoine!

903
00:38:57,168 --> 00:38:59,253
Right to the outdoor shower!
- I just drive 'em.

904
00:38:59,295 --> 00:39:00,713
I'm not
a wash and detail service.

905
00:39:00,755 --> 00:39:03,090
- Yeah, yeah,
God forbid you prove useful.

906
00:39:03,132 --> 00:39:04,633
Ah, good timing, Ms. Emma.

907
00:39:04,717 --> 00:39:07,386
Time for you and me to do
the Lord's work. This way.

908
00:39:07,428 --> 00:39:09,263
- Ooh, God, what is that?

909
00:39:09,347 --> 00:39:13,059
- Okay, big breath.
- [breathes deeply]

910
00:39:13,100 --> 00:39:17,063
- All right, lungs sound great.
Can you lean forward for me?

911
00:39:22,610 --> 00:39:26,280
Uh, did you hit your back
when you fell down the stairs?

912
00:39:26,364 --> 00:39:27,448
- No.

913
00:39:27,490 --> 00:39:29,283
- Okay, there's
a pretty good bruise here.

914
00:39:29,367 --> 00:39:31,243
- I got knocked down in soccer.

915
00:39:33,287 --> 00:39:36,082
- Oh, no, you broke your wrist
last year, too, huh?

916
00:39:36,123 --> 00:39:38,876
- Fell off my skateboard.
Got a purple cast.

917
00:39:38,959 --> 00:39:40,461
- Cool color.

918
00:39:40,544 --> 00:39:42,463
- And you were treated
at West Penn?

919
00:39:42,505 --> 00:39:44,924
Is that your closest hospital?

920
00:39:44,965 --> 00:39:46,634
- Uh, I guess
that would make sense.

921
00:39:46,717 --> 00:39:48,636
I've only been dating her dad
a few months.

922
00:39:48,719 --> 00:39:51,430
Uh, he lived in Garfield
before the divorce.

923
00:39:51,472 --> 00:39:53,933
- And, uh, what about these?
- Soccer.

924
00:39:53,974 --> 00:39:55,643
- Do you wear shin guards?

925
00:39:55,684 --> 00:39:57,311
- My dad ordered them.

926
00:39:57,353 --> 00:40:00,648
- Okay. What does your dad do
for work?

927
00:40:00,731 --> 00:40:03,526
- He draws buildings,
but he's not an architect.

928
00:40:03,609 --> 00:40:05,986
- He's a draftsman
for a firm in Newcastle.

929
00:40:06,028 --> 00:40:10,366
- Okay. Um, Kylie,
any chance you can pee?

930
00:40:10,449 --> 00:40:12,827
- Mm, maybe.
- Okay.

931
00:40:12,868 --> 00:40:14,829
Well, with the bruise
on your back,

932
00:40:14,912 --> 00:40:16,414
we need to check your urine.

933
00:40:16,497 --> 00:40:18,082
- Okay.
- Okay.

934
00:40:21,001 --> 00:40:23,337
- Did you eat anything
out of the ordinary recently?

935
00:40:23,379 --> 00:40:24,755
- No.

936
00:40:24,839 --> 00:40:27,174
Sorry, but we went
through this a bunch.

937
00:40:27,258 --> 00:40:28,843
Don't you people communicate?

938
00:40:28,926 --> 00:40:30,761
You're the third nurse
we've told her story to.

939
00:40:30,845 --> 00:40:32,096
- I'm a doctor.

940
00:40:32,179 --> 00:40:35,141
- Richard, would you go
to the cafeteria

941
00:40:35,182 --> 00:40:37,518
and get me
a large chamomile tea?

942
00:40:37,601 --> 00:40:39,687
What can we get you, my dear?

943
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:42,106
- I'm fine, thank you.

944
00:40:44,066 --> 00:40:45,901
- Yeah, I'll be right back.

945
00:40:49,738 --> 00:40:52,074
- Please excuse my son.

946
00:40:52,158 --> 00:40:54,034
Ever since my husband died,

947
00:40:54,076 --> 00:40:56,412
he's been
a little overprotective.

948
00:40:56,495 --> 00:40:58,497
- I think it's sweet.

949
00:40:58,539 --> 00:41:01,417
You're very lucky to have him.
- I am.

950
00:41:03,002 --> 00:41:06,881
- Are you still taking your
loperamide and cholestyramine?

951
00:41:06,922 --> 00:41:09,675
- Um, mm, not so much.

952
00:41:09,717 --> 00:41:11,385
- No?

953
00:41:11,427 --> 00:41:13,762
That may be why you're
experiencing the abdominal pain

954
00:41:13,846 --> 00:41:15,389
and vomiting.

955
00:41:15,473 --> 00:41:17,641
When did you stop taking
your meds?

956
00:41:17,725 --> 00:41:19,894
- Last winter or so.

957
00:41:21,896 --> 00:41:25,483
My neighbor Helen and I bingo
in Wintersville.

958
00:41:25,566 --> 00:41:28,194
There's a dispensary next door.

959
00:41:28,235 --> 00:41:30,237
And we got some marijuana.

960
00:41:30,279 --> 00:41:34,492
And I swear
it cleared everything up.

961
00:41:34,575 --> 00:41:38,078
A few cookies
every few hours...

962
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:40,748
and no more pain.

963
00:41:40,789 --> 00:41:43,250
- "A few cookies"?

964
00:41:43,334 --> 00:41:45,419
How many are you eating
in a day?

965
00:41:45,461 --> 00:41:47,463
- Oh, uh...

966
00:41:47,546 --> 00:41:50,132
I don't know.

967
00:41:50,216 --> 00:41:55,095
Probably a dozen or so,
but they're not very big.

968
00:41:55,137 --> 00:41:57,097
- There have been some studies

969
00:41:57,139 --> 00:41:59,266
that show
an added dementia risk

970
00:41:59,350 --> 00:42:02,353
with marijuana use
in older adults.

971
00:42:03,729 --> 00:42:06,398
- Well, that sucks
for old people.

972
00:42:06,440 --> 00:42:07,733
[urine trickling]

973
00:42:07,775 --> 00:42:09,652
- Kylie,
there are some questions

974
00:42:09,735 --> 00:42:13,739
that we ask every kid
who comes in with an injury.

975
00:42:13,781 --> 00:42:15,282
- Like what?

976
00:42:15,324 --> 00:42:18,744
- Like, sometimes the people
that you live with

977
00:42:18,786 --> 00:42:23,749
can get so mad that
they hit you or hurt you,

978
00:42:23,791 --> 00:42:25,960
maybe without even meaning to.

979
00:42:26,001 --> 00:42:27,419
Does that ever happen?

980
00:42:27,461 --> 00:42:29,880
- No, not to me.

981
00:42:29,964 --> 00:42:32,132
- Or sometimes kids
can get punished

982
00:42:32,174 --> 00:42:33,759
for doing something wrong

983
00:42:33,801 --> 00:42:37,304
with maybe a spanking
or a belt.

984
00:42:37,346 --> 00:42:39,974
- I get time-outs.

985
00:42:40,015 --> 00:42:42,101
- How about at school, Kylie?

986
00:42:42,142 --> 00:42:45,563
You know, is anybody being,
you know, mean or bullying you?

987
00:42:45,646 --> 00:42:48,232
- No, I have lots of friends.

988
00:42:51,360 --> 00:42:52,319
- [clears throat]

989
00:42:52,361 --> 00:42:54,947
[toilet flushing]

990
00:42:57,783 --> 00:43:00,327
[water running]

991
00:43:09,044 --> 00:43:11,171
- Yo, Whitaker.

992
00:43:11,213 --> 00:43:14,008
- Dr. Langdon.

993
00:43:14,049 --> 00:43:17,803
- You, uh, meet Louie here?
- Uh, yeah, yeah, many times.

994
00:43:17,845 --> 00:43:20,180
Good morning, Mr. Cloverfield.
- What's up, Doc?

995
00:43:20,222 --> 00:43:22,349
- Where's your new resident
badge buddy, buddy?

996
00:43:22,433 --> 00:43:26,687
- Oh, uh, yeah, they--they
didn't give me a new one yet.

997
00:43:26,770 --> 00:43:28,022
- Bummer.

998
00:43:28,105 --> 00:43:29,607
You and, uh,
your ducklings got time

999
00:43:29,690 --> 00:43:31,317
to tap a six-pack
off our friend here?

1000
00:43:31,358 --> 00:43:33,861
Louie needs a paracentesis.
- Yeah, sure.

1001
00:43:33,902 --> 00:43:35,821
It's good to see you
again, Louie--

1002
00:43:35,863 --> 00:43:37,531
not in this condition,
of course.

1003
00:43:37,615 --> 00:43:38,616
How you feeling?

1004
00:43:38,699 --> 00:43:41,994
- Feeling like I need
a drink pretty soon.

1005
00:43:42,036 --> 00:43:43,829
- Okay, well,
first things first--

1006
00:43:43,871 --> 00:43:46,040
we are going to make you
more comfortable

1007
00:43:46,123 --> 00:43:47,708
by draining some fluid.

1008
00:43:47,750 --> 00:43:49,376
- You're gonna take off
a big volume,

1009
00:43:49,460 --> 00:43:52,546
so start with 60 grams
of 25% albumin IV.

1010
00:43:52,588 --> 00:43:55,090
You tapped him before?
- No.

1011
00:43:55,174 --> 00:43:57,593
- That's okay, Doc.
I'll talk him through it.

1012
00:43:57,676 --> 00:43:59,428
- [chuckles]
Okay.

1013
00:44:01,055 --> 00:44:04,475
- Sats 99 on room air,
normal BP, normal pulse.

1014
00:44:07,269 --> 00:44:08,896
- Oh, well hydrated.

1015
00:44:08,937 --> 00:44:10,606
- No obvious source
of infection.

1016
00:44:10,689 --> 00:44:13,609
Could be a benign virus,
but can't assume that.

1017
00:44:13,692 --> 00:44:16,236
- Is this the abandoned baby?
- Mm-hmm.

1018
00:44:16,320 --> 00:44:18,614
- How old?
- How old do you think she is?

1019
00:44:18,697 --> 00:44:22,076
- Month, month and a half?
- It's tricky.

1020
00:44:22,117 --> 00:44:25,412
Under 28 days is a Safe Haven
drop-off, no questions asked.

1021
00:44:25,496 --> 00:44:28,916
But over 28 days is a crime,
child abandonment.

1022
00:44:28,957 --> 00:44:31,085
- How should we assess?

1023
00:44:31,126 --> 00:44:34,171
- Under 28 days gets everything,
including an LP.

1024
00:44:34,254 --> 00:44:36,507
But over 28 days,

1025
00:44:36,590 --> 00:44:38,676
you can follow PECARN
or Step-by-step

1026
00:44:38,759 --> 00:44:41,428
to determine risk of invasive
bacterial infection.

1027
00:44:41,470 --> 00:44:42,930
- This baby looks great.

1028
00:44:42,971 --> 00:44:44,890
- But we know nothing
about the birth history.

1029
00:44:44,932 --> 00:44:48,686
Did Mom have group B strep,
active HSV lesions?

1030
00:44:48,769 --> 00:44:52,773
- We should assume the worst.
Put in your orders, Dr. Mohan.

1031
00:44:52,815 --> 00:44:54,775
- CBC...
[sighs]

1032
00:44:54,858 --> 00:44:57,277
Blood culture, chem panel,

1033
00:44:57,361 --> 00:45:00,155
chest X-ray, UA and culture,

1034
00:45:00,239 --> 00:45:02,408
respiratory virus panel.

1035
00:45:02,449 --> 00:45:06,662
Check inflammatory markers--
procalcitonin and CRP--

1036
00:45:06,745 --> 00:45:08,789
then take it from there.

1037
00:45:08,831 --> 00:45:10,958
- That sounds like a plan.

1038
00:45:11,041 --> 00:45:13,127
- Dr. Robby,
Santos needs you in North 1.

1039
00:45:13,210 --> 00:45:14,670
- Okay, I'll be right there.

1040
00:45:14,753 --> 00:45:17,214
If you'll excuse me,
if you'll excuse me.

1041
00:45:19,550 --> 00:45:23,762
- Mind if I join with?
- Uh, no, I got this.

1042
00:45:23,804 --> 00:45:25,472
- It will help me
get a better sense

1043
00:45:25,556 --> 00:45:27,725
of how you like to do things
by seeing you in action

1044
00:45:27,808 --> 00:45:29,268
with the residents.

1045
00:45:29,309 --> 00:45:31,019
- Be my guest.

1046
00:45:33,731 --> 00:45:35,941
- I will be right back.

1047
00:45:41,029 --> 00:45:43,741
Um, nine-year-old
Kylie Connors--

1048
00:45:43,824 --> 00:45:45,826
took a tumble running up
the stairs this morning.

1049
00:45:45,868 --> 00:45:47,578
Chin lac and fractured tooth.

1050
00:45:47,661 --> 00:45:49,830
Brought in by her dad's
girlfriend, Gina.

1051
00:45:49,872 --> 00:45:52,958
Supposedly, the dad had
to leave early for work.

1052
00:45:53,000 --> 00:45:55,711
Kylie has a lot of bruising
on her body--

1053
00:45:55,794 --> 00:45:57,337
some old, some new.

1054
00:45:57,379 --> 00:46:00,883
- Are you concerned
about non-accidental trauma?

1055
00:46:00,966 --> 00:46:02,509
- Or worse.

1056
00:46:02,593 --> 00:46:05,012
Her urine sample shows
gross hematuria,

1057
00:46:05,053 --> 00:46:08,182
which could be renal injury,
or vaginal bleeding from trauma

1058
00:46:08,265 --> 00:46:10,476
could have contaminated
the specimen.

1059
00:46:10,517 --> 00:46:13,353
- Lots to consider.
What's your plan?

1060
00:46:13,437 --> 00:46:16,023
- Labs, ultrasound,
social-work consult,

1061
00:46:16,106 --> 00:46:18,066
then decide
if this is reportable.

1062
00:46:18,150 --> 00:46:19,568
- Mm-hmm.

1063
00:46:22,780 --> 00:46:24,531
- Let me know
if you can feel this.

1064
00:46:24,615 --> 00:46:26,366
- No.
Am I supposed to?

1065
00:46:26,450 --> 00:46:27,659
- No.

1066
00:46:31,872 --> 00:46:33,999
- Your hair's
such a pretty color.

1067
00:46:34,041 --> 00:46:35,209
- Thank you.

1068
00:46:35,250 --> 00:46:37,711
- Reminds me of my mom.

1069
00:46:37,753 --> 00:46:41,006
She died when I was nine.
- Oh, I'm sorry about that.

1070
00:46:41,048 --> 00:46:43,550
- She wore glasses
like you, too.

1071
00:46:43,634 --> 00:46:45,552
Weird.

1072
00:46:45,636 --> 00:46:48,972
What do you like to do
when you're not working?

1073
00:46:49,056 --> 00:46:51,391
- Um, spend some time
with my sister,

1074
00:46:51,475 --> 00:46:53,227
read.

1075
00:46:53,268 --> 00:46:54,812
- Do you drink coffee?

1076
00:46:54,895 --> 00:46:58,440
- Uh, no, I avoid caffeine.

1077
00:46:58,524 --> 00:46:59,566
- Me too.

1078
00:46:59,650 --> 00:47:01,235
Um...

1079
00:47:01,318 --> 00:47:03,320
what about boba?

1080
00:47:04,863 --> 00:47:08,367
- I love boba.
[chuckles]

1081
00:47:10,994 --> 00:47:12,913
- I'd offer you some gum,
but it's laced with nicotine.

1082
00:47:12,996 --> 00:47:14,790
Trying to quit smoking.

1083
00:47:14,873 --> 00:47:17,417
- [sighs]
That's good.

1084
00:47:17,459 --> 00:47:19,127
- Verdict's still out.

1085
00:47:19,211 --> 00:47:21,547
Get in all those nooks
and crannies, Mr. Digby.

1086
00:47:21,588 --> 00:47:23,423
Your skin will thank you.

1087
00:47:23,465 --> 00:47:25,300
We all will.

1088
00:47:25,384 --> 00:47:29,346
- Oh, I could stay
in here all day.

1089
00:47:29,429 --> 00:47:31,598
- It's a miracle what little
soap, water, and human decency

1090
00:47:31,682 --> 00:47:33,600
can do sometimes.

1091
00:47:33,684 --> 00:47:35,060
Don't forget the ears, pal.

1092
00:47:35,102 --> 00:47:37,020
Don't want anything
growing in there either.

1093
00:47:37,104 --> 00:47:38,605
- Okay. Okay.

1094
00:47:38,689 --> 00:47:40,566
- What's your story, Emma?

1095
00:47:40,607 --> 00:47:43,443
What'd your parents do?
Got siblings? Boyfriend?

1096
00:47:43,485 --> 00:47:46,363
Ever kill a man in a bar fight?

1097
00:47:46,446 --> 00:47:47,739
- Uh...

1098
00:47:49,616 --> 00:47:52,661
[indistinct chatter]
- [sighs]

1099
00:47:57,165 --> 00:47:59,459
So, Mr. Williams,

1100
00:47:59,501 --> 00:48:01,837
sometimes a fracture
of the scaphoid bone

1101
00:48:01,920 --> 00:48:03,755
doesn't show up on day one.

1102
00:48:03,797 --> 00:48:07,467
So my advice to be safe is
wear a splint for two weeks

1103
00:48:07,509 --> 00:48:09,636
and then come back
for a repeat X-ray,

1104
00:48:09,720 --> 00:48:11,471
and we'll see
if it's really there.

1105
00:48:11,513 --> 00:48:13,765
- [sighs]

1106
00:48:13,807 --> 00:48:16,351
My knee hurts.

1107
00:48:16,435 --> 00:48:19,688
- Oh, did you hurt your knee?

1108
00:48:19,771 --> 00:48:21,523
- I must have.

1109
00:48:28,071 --> 00:48:30,115
What is this?

1110
00:48:30,157 --> 00:48:32,993
- That's, um, for your wrist.

1111
00:48:33,076 --> 00:48:35,954
- And I need this?

1112
00:48:35,996 --> 00:48:38,665
- Well, um, if it's a, uh--

1113
00:48:38,749 --> 00:48:40,751
John, can you?

1114
00:48:40,834 --> 00:48:43,337
If it's a fracture
and we don't immobilize,

1115
00:48:43,378 --> 00:48:45,589
the bone might not heal
properly.

1116
00:48:47,549 --> 00:48:51,345
- [sighs]
How long is this gonna take?

1117
00:48:51,386 --> 00:48:53,805
- Just a few minutes
to fit you.

1118
00:48:56,683 --> 00:48:59,186
- Is this place new?

1119
00:49:01,897 --> 00:49:05,901
- Mr. Williams,
do you know what day it is?

1120
00:49:09,446 --> 00:49:11,907
- It's Taco Tuesday.

1121
00:49:11,990 --> 00:49:14,242
- Do you know where you are?

1122
00:49:15,744 --> 00:49:18,914
- Yeah, I'm in the slowest
medical clinic in the West.

1123
00:49:18,997 --> 00:49:21,375
- Do you know what year it is?

1124
00:49:21,458 --> 00:49:23,543
- How about you just
fix my wrist

1125
00:49:23,585 --> 00:49:27,714
and you stop
with all the questions?

1126
00:49:27,798 --> 00:49:30,884
I am not here to be profiled.

1127
00:49:35,055 --> 00:49:37,891
- Yeah, of course.

1128
00:49:37,975 --> 00:49:40,102
I'll be back, okay?

1129
00:49:43,480 --> 00:49:46,274
[inaudible speech]

1130
00:49:50,821 --> 00:49:53,490
- All right, Mr. Digby,
looking better already.

1131
00:49:53,573 --> 00:49:55,409
Certainly smell
a little better.

1132
00:49:55,492 --> 00:49:56,910
- Do you have any conditioner?

1133
00:49:56,994 --> 00:49:59,079
- Let's get you dried off
and back inside.

1134
00:49:59,121 --> 00:50:00,747
Get him gowned up
and put him in South 21,

1135
00:50:00,789 --> 00:50:01,915
across from the old nun.

1136
00:50:01,957 --> 00:50:05,210
Maybe some of her divine grace
will wear off on him.

1137
00:50:05,252 --> 00:50:06,545
Who do we have here?

1138
00:50:06,586 --> 00:50:09,423
- Evelyn Bostick, 81,
with Alzheimer's.

1139
00:50:09,506 --> 00:50:11,717
We brought her husband in
about 30 minutes ago.

1140
00:50:11,758 --> 00:50:13,301
She was supposed
to get a ride in,

1141
00:50:13,385 --> 00:50:14,970
but they found her
wandering in traffic.

1142
00:50:15,053 --> 00:50:17,055
- Where are we?
- You're at the hospital, dear.

1143
00:50:17,097 --> 00:50:18,432
- I'm not sick.

1144
00:50:18,473 --> 00:50:20,434
- We brought you
to see your husband.

1145
00:50:20,475 --> 00:50:22,436
- Oh, no.
Is Ethan okay?

1146
00:50:22,477 --> 00:50:24,980
- I'll be in to chat with you,
Mrs. Bostick.

1147
00:50:25,063 --> 00:50:27,566
He died.

1148
00:50:27,607 --> 00:50:29,443
What you got, Amboy?

1149
00:50:29,484 --> 00:50:32,612
- Allen Billings--had a pallet
fall onto his outstretched arm.

1150
00:50:32,696 --> 00:50:35,073
Looks like an open dislocation.
- Yeah, that's got to hurt.

1151
00:50:35,115 --> 00:50:36,783
Don't worry, sir.
We got you.

1152
00:50:36,825 --> 00:50:39,953
- [breathing heavily]

1153
00:50:42,873 --> 00:50:46,043
- How's she doing?
- Seems happy enough.

1154
00:50:46,126 --> 00:50:48,045
- Yeah. Hi.
- Hi.

1155
00:50:48,128 --> 00:50:50,797
Just got a quick
point-of-care CBC.

1156
00:50:50,881 --> 00:50:52,716
- [chuckles]

1157
00:50:57,304 --> 00:50:59,306
Is there a problem?

1158
00:51:02,809 --> 00:51:04,603
Did I miss something?

1159
00:51:04,644 --> 00:51:07,647
Dr. Al? Dr. Al?

1160
00:51:11,610 --> 00:51:14,321
[Yvonne Fair's
"Say Yeah Yeah"]

1161
00:51:14,362 --> 00:51:17,616
- ♪ I've been hurt ♪

1162
00:51:17,657 --> 00:51:20,368
♪ But I refuse to cry ♪

1163
00:51:22,120 --> 00:51:24,998
♪ I've been hurt, baby ♪

1164
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:27,584
♪ But I refuse to cry ♪

1165
00:51:29,544 --> 00:51:31,046
♪ It's so true ♪

1166
00:51:31,129 --> 00:51:32,839
♪ You know, you know,
you know ♪

1167
00:51:32,923 --> 00:51:35,008
♪ You know I can't lie ♪

1168
00:51:35,092 --> 00:51:37,677
♪ And I said,
yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1169
00:51:37,761 --> 00:51:39,805
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1170
00:51:39,846 --> 00:51:41,681
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1171
00:51:41,723 --> 00:51:43,683
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1172
00:51:43,725 --> 00:51:46,812
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah, baby ♪
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah! ♪

1173
00:51:46,853 --> 00:51:48,688
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1174
00:51:48,730 --> 00:51:50,607
- ♪ Yeah, yeah, yeah ♪

1175
00:51:50,690 --> 00:51:52,359
- ♪ I won't cry ♪

1176
00:51:52,442 --> 00:51:56,113
♪ Oh, you know I won't cry ♪

1177
00:51:56,196 --> 00:51:57,823
♪ I said ♪

1178
00:51:57,864 --> 00:52:00,700
♪ I won't cry,
baby, baby, baby ♪

1179
00:52:00,742 --> 00:52:03,453
♪ You know I won't cry ♪

1180
00:52:03,537 --> 00:52:06,832
♪ You know, you know
I won't cry, baby ♪

1181
00:52:06,873 --> 00:52:10,377
♪ I'm telling you,
I won't cry ♪

1182
00:52:10,460 --> 00:52:13,588
♪ Oh, I don't lie ♪

1183
00:52:13,672 --> 00:52:17,217
♪ Oh, and I refuse to cry ♪

1184
00:52:17,300 --> 00:52:19,427
♪ And I said ♪

1185
00:52:19,511 --> 00:52:21,555
♪ From time to time ♪

1186
00:52:21,596 --> 00:52:24,432
♪ Your friends,
they treat you so cold ♪

1187
00:52:24,516 --> 00:52:28,603
♪ Your friends, you know they
act better, yeah, yeah ♪

1188
00:52:28,687 --> 00:52:32,190
♪ When you have that gold ♪

1189
00:52:32,232 --> 00:52:34,609
♪ Oh, I won't lie ♪

1190
00:52:34,693 --> 00:52:36,736
♪ You know, you know,
you know ♪

